A||N||4||kornholio||stalling right after starting||||11
Z||000000||kornholio||11-14-1999||07:35 PM||||For the last 2 days, my car has started right up but stalls right away.  This only happens for the first start of the day, first turn of the key.  The second turn of the key is always successful in starting with no stalling.<P>And just 2 days ago, I was driving around stopping at a few places.  On my way home, I was downshifting with my automatic to slow the car down (I do that once in a while).  At a really long stop light I shifted into neutral and the engine stalled right away.  This only used to happen before I changed the EGR solenoid.  I start it up and start driving again.  Going down the street, I shift into neutral to see if it would stall again.  It did.  I did this 2 other times and it stalled both times.  I got home and when I shifted into park, it stalled.  I decided to check it out the next day since it was 2 am.  Now the weird thing is, the next morning and all of today, I didn't get this problem at all.<P>Should either of these problems cause some concern?<BR>What should I check on the engine?<P>Thanks.||||reg||11
Z||000001||dogtired||11-15-1999||01:47 PM||||COOL Automatic Shut-Off! Where can I get one?   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  <BR>All kidding aside, I would think that would set a code, I would start there. It really sounds like a vacuum line is off, which one?<BR>Good luck.||||reg||6
Z||000002||WKDFIRO||11-17-1999||03:51 PM||||Intermittant, no pattern at all stalling. If you have already checked the EGR valve to make sure its not sticking, the Idle Air Control valve is clean, AND you have an automatic you MIGHT have a lock up solenoid sticking. But make sure you have checked everything else. The solenoid is in the tranny and a bit of a chore to get at.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Linx||11-18-1999||12:31 AM||||Man- I've been battling that problem for months.  I've replaced the tcc and egr solenoids.  cap and rotor.  plug wires. all the manifold gaskets. the iac.<P>and then, this two inch piece of vaccuum hose...just below #5 in this drawing.  located on the lower manifold and just to the side of the cold start injector.<P>   <IMG SRC="http://members.aol.com/linxramblr/tbody.jpg">   <P>I'd looked at that hose before, and it was always snug.  but snug aint good enough. its gotta be tight!  And dont forget the O-ring inside the throttle body where #5 goes in.  When reassembling it, remember to push #5 deep into the throttle body for a good seal.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Linx (edited 11-18-1999).]||||reg||3
Z||000004||Cliff Pennock||11-18-1999||04:34 AM||||Thanks Lynx, I have the exact same problem since a few weeks (never did it before). I will check out that hose today.||||reg||14
